module gunbc.host_diagnostic_channel


// WHOSE ERROR IS THIS? Five distinct authorities emitted diagnostics during
// the 2026-08-11 fleet incident, and they were being read as one
// undifferentiated stream of "errors". They are not one thing: they have
// different owners, different registries, different stability guarantees,
// and — the operationally decisive part — different TRANSPORTS, so a
// consumer wired to one channel is structurally blind to the others.
//
// Modeling the emitter is not taxonomy for its own sake. A DMTF message id
// is a versioned identifier in a published registry and can be decoded
// offline; an Ampere postcode is an opaque integer whose meaning we do not
// possess; a vendor shell script's English sentence has no registry at all
// and can change wording in any firmware release. Rendering all three as
// "error: <string>" throws away exactly the facts that decide whether a
// diagnostic can be matched on, ranged over, or only shown to a human.
// DMTF-standardized Redfish message registries. Identifiers carry their
// own registry name AND version, e.g. "B16.0.InternalError".
// On OpenBmcPhosphorNamespace:
// OpenBMC's D-Bus error namespace, e.g.
// "xyz.openbmc_project.State.Shutdown.Power.Error.Blackout". Structured,
// hierarchical, enumerable from the running BMC.
// On AmpereScpConsoleTokenStream:
// Ampere SCP/SMpro/PMpro console tokens (P012, E588, P029), modeled
// upstream in extdeps.ampere.scp_diagnostic. Opaque to this repository:
// no codebook has been obtained, so a token is an IDENTITY we compare and
// count, never a meaning we claim.
// On AmpereStructuredBootProgressInterface:
// The STRUCTURED boot-progress interface: status, stage and payload from
// smpro-misc. A different surface from the token stream above, and a
// strictly richer one — it localized the 2026-08-11 stall to a stage while
// the console emitted only an undecodable lexeme. Fusing the two would
// have hidden that the structured source existed at all.
// On AmpereDramReferenceCode:
// Ampere DRAM reference-code training diagnostics, e.g.
// "MC3:R0: WRLVL Err=0 Stat=0x0 NibbleMsk=0xfc0", "SK[0]: 001200ff".
// Structured per memory controller and rank, but privately defined.
// On VendorScriptFreeText:
// Free text printed by vendor shell scripts, e.g. firmware_update.sh's
// "Invalid ExtendedVersion: primary-all. Please check MANIFEST file!".
// No registry, no identifier, no stability contract. Human-readable only.
type DiagnosticAuthority
  = RedfishMessageRegistry
  | OpenBmcPhosphorNamespace
  | AmpereScpConsoleTokenStream
  | AmpereStructuredBootProgressInterface
  | AmpereDramReferenceCode
  | VendorScriptFreeText

// HOW WAS IT READ? This axis is why the incident took as long as it did.
// The convergence surface is Redfish, and Redfish could see almost none of
// the diagnostics that mattered.
// Redfish over HTTPS: reachable by any authenticated convergence run.
// On BmcConsoleLogViaSsh:
// The BMC's captured host serial console (/var/log/obmc-console.log),
// reached by authenticated BMC SSH.
// On SmproSysfsViaSsh:
// SMpro/PMpro sysfs on the BMC. Note this is a TRANSPORT, not an
// authority: the same underlying data is reachable by other handlers
// where an image implements them (a correct D-Bus/Redfish projection,
// the dedicated SCP console, or Ampere's OEM IPMI register read).
// On BmcJournalViaSsh:
// The BMC's systemd journal. Requires authenticated BMC SSH.
type DiagnosticTransport
  = RedfishHttps
  | BmcConsoleLogViaSsh
  | SmproSysfsViaSsh
  | BmcJournalViaSsh

// Can a machine act on this diagnostic, or can it only be shown to a person?
// The distinction is not cosmetic: it decides whether convergence may branch
// on the diagnostic at all.
// Identifier resolves in a registry this repository can obtain and cite.
// On OpaqueStableIdentifier:
// Stable identity, meaning unavailable. Comparable and countable, but its
// SEMANTICS may not be asserted. E588 is here. Note what this does NOT
// claim: the E prefix places the token in a namespace, it does not make it
// an error and it does not make it fatal — E559 and E571 appear on a
// same-family machine that boots normally. That E588 is followed by a reset
// on srv3 and srv4 is an observed transition recorded in a fleet receipt,
// never a property read off the lexeme.
// On UnstructuredProse:
// No identifier at all. Matching on the prose is matching on a wording the
// vendor may change without notice.
type DiagnosticDecodability
  = DecodableFromPublishedRegistry { registry: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | OpaqueStableIdentifier
  | UnstructuredProse

// On raw:
// Verbatim, as emitted. Never normalized, because normalizing a code we
// cannot decode can only lose information.
type HostDiagnostic {
  host: product.placement_supply.HostIdentity
  authority: DiagnosticAuthority
  transport: DiagnosticTransport
  decodability: DiagnosticDecodability
  raw: std.types.NonEmptyStr
  observed_at: std.types.EpochMs
}

// Whether a diagnostic may be READ by a given convergence run, derived from
// what that run can reach. An unreachable channel is not an absence of
// errors — it is an absence of observation, and the two must never collapse
// (DESIGN section 5: empty-observation narrow).
type DiagnosticChannelReachability
  = ChannelReachable
  | ChannelRequiresBmcSsh
  | ChannelReachabilityUnobserved

fn diagnostic_channel_reachability(
  transport: DiagnosticTransport,
  bmc_ssh_available: Bool,
) -> DiagnosticChannelReachability {
  match transport {
    RedfishHttps => ChannelReachable
    BmcConsoleLogViaSsh =>
      if bmc_ssh_available { ChannelReachable } else { ChannelRequiresBmcSsh }
    SmproSysfsViaSsh =>
      if bmc_ssh_available { ChannelReachable } else { ChannelRequiresBmcSsh }
    BmcJournalViaSsh =>
      if bmc_ssh_available { ChannelReachable } else { ChannelRequiresBmcSsh }
  }
}

// A MISSING PROJECTION IS NOT MISSING DATA. Redfish returning None, an empty
// LogService, or a BootProgress of "None" says something about the PROJECTION
// this firmware image implements, and nothing whatever about whether the
// source data exists. Proven live on 2026-08-11: srv3's Redfish HostLogger
// reported zero entries and BootProgress None while the console file on disk
// held every boot cycle and smpro-misc boot_progress returned an advancing
// structured value — and no ampere-boot-progress service was installed on the
// image at all, so the projection was not incomplete, it was absent.
//
// An earlier revision of this module concluded from those same observations
// that these channels are "BMC SSH only". That inferred a property of the
// SOURCE from a property of one vendor image's PROJECTION, which is the
// empty-observation narrow this module exists to prevent.
type DiagnosticProjectionStanding
  = ProjectionPresentAndPopulated
  | ProjectionUnavailableOrIncomplete { projection: std.types.NonEmptyStr }
  | SourceConfirmedAbsent { source: std.types.NonEmptyStr, evidence: std.types.NonEmptyStr }

fn projection_absence_establishes_source_absence() -> Bool {
  false
}

// A convergence run that reads ONLY Redfish and finds nothing has not
// established that the host is healthy. Both hosts in this incident sat in
// deterministic reset loops while Redfish reported BootProgress=None, an
// empty HostLogger, and TaskState=Exception with no cause. This function
// exists so that "no diagnostics found" carries its own coverage caveat.
fn redfish_only_observation_is_complete() -> Bool {
  false
}

data host_diagnostic_channel_incident_receipt: String = "Executed 2026-08-11 against srv3 (192.168.1.192) and srv4 (192.168.1.195), ALTRAD8UD-1L2T / Ampere Altra Max M128-30. FIVE authorities were read, and the three most diagnostic ones were unreachable from Redfish. (1) RedfishMessageRegistry: 'B16.0.InternalError' from Bios.ResetBios, the only identifier of the five that names its own registry and version. (2) OpenBmcPhosphorNamespace: 'xyz.openbmc_project.State.Shutdown.Power.Error.Blackout' in the Redfish EventLog. (3) AmpereHostFirmwarePostCode: the repeating signature P012 -> E588 -> P029, read ONLY from /var/log/obmc-console.log over authenticated BMC SSH — the Redfish HostLogger LogService returned Members@odata.count=0 for the same period, so a Redfish-only consumer saw an empty console while the disk file held every cycle. (4) AmpereDramReferenceCode: 'MC3:R0: WRLVL Err=0 Stat=0x0 NibbleMsk=0xfc0', 'SK[0]: 001200ff', 'MC[0..7]: 00f100b4', 'DDR initialization failure. Can't fall back!' — same console-only transport; this is the evidence that identified the BIOS 2.06 memory reference code as the fault and it was invisible to Redfish. (5) VendorScriptFreeText: firmware_update.sh's 'Invalid ExtendedVersion: primary-all. Please check MANIFEST file!' in the BMC journal, which was the ONLY statement of why a firmware push failed — Redfish reported TaskState=Exception with the message 'The task with Id 0 has completed with errors' and no cause whatsoever. THE STANDING CONSEQUENCE: any convergence that consumes only Redfish can observe THAT something failed and never WHY, so BMC SSH is a required diagnostic transport for this fleet rather than a debugging convenience. E588 and P012 remain OpaqueStableIdentifier: no ASRock or Ampere postcode table has been obtained (both published manuals are PDFs whose text this repository could not extract, and the vendor support site is behind bot protection), so their meaning is UNCLAIMED. They may be compared and counted; they may not be interpreted."
